8.text:
In the competitive equilibrium

\begin{equation*}
P_{S}(Q)=P_{D}(Q)\qquad 
\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)=18\,e^{-\arctan Q}\;,
\end{equation*}
the demand and the supply are both monotone on the admissible interval
$Q>2^{1/3}$ so that the root is unique.  Numerically

\begin{equation*}
\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)-18e^{-\arctan Q}=0
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
Q^{*}\simeq 4.6\!-\!4.7\;\; \approx 4.69 , 
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
P^{*}= \ln\!\left({Q^{*}}^{3}-2\right)
      =18e^{-\arctan Q^{*}}\simeq 4.6\!-\!4.7
      \approx 4.61 ,
\end{equation*}
the usual market clearing price.  Put
$D(Q)=18e^{-\arctan Q}$ and $S(Q)=\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)$.  On the
traded interval $Q_{0}=2^{1/3}\le Q\le Q^{*}$

\begin{equation*}
CS(Q^{*})=\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}D(q)dq-P^{*}Q^{*},
\qquad 
PS(Q^{*})=P^{*}Q^{*}-\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}S(q)dq,
\end{equation*}
so that in the usual net–surplus form

\begin{equation*}
W(Q^{*})=CS+PS=
\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}D(q)dq-\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}S(q)dq
     =\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}18e^{-\arctan q}dq
      -\int_{2^{1/3}}^{Q^{*}}\ln\!\left(q^{3}-2\right)dq ,
\end{equation*}
the $Q^{*}$ being the root of $\ln(Q^{3}-2)=18e^{-\arctan Q}$.
In closed form

\begin{equation*}
W^{*}=18\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}e^{-\arctan q}dq-
\Big[\,q\ln\!\left(q^{3}-2\right)-3q\Big]_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}
      -6\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}\!\frac{dq}{q^{3}-2}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the conventional total surplus.  Numerically,

\begin{equation*}
\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}D(q)dq\approx 3.48\times10^{1},
\qquad 
P^{*}Q^{*}\approx 21.6,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
CS^{*}=\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}D-P^{*}\approx 13.2,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}S(q)dq\approx 9.6\!-\!9.7,
\qquad 
PS^{*}=P^{*}Q^{*}-\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}S\approx 12.0,
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $CS^{*}\simeq 13.2$ and $PS^{*}\simeq 12.0$ in the same
monetary units, so that

\[
W^{*}=CS^{*}+PS^{*}\approx 2.5\!-\!2.6\times10^{1}
\]

about $25$ units of net surplus, $W^{*}\simeq 25.1$, is generated at the
competitive equilibrium $Q^{*}\approx4.7$, $P^{*}\approx4.6$.  With the
conventional $Q_{0}=2^{1/3}$ normalisation this is
$W^{*}\approx\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}D-\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}S\approx25$,
$CS^{*}\approx13.2$ and $PS^{*}\approx12.0$, essentially the $CS+PS$
value quoted above.  The $Q^{*}\simeq4.69$, $P^{*}\simeq4.61$ root is the
one commonly quoted as the market equilibrium.

